From 5e9a39e915b262da003e1d200c8554c36713bd65 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 00:13:21 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 05/25] l2r-local: step D (R2b): Mpz clones and drops through
 compiler-emitted rcgmp glue that inlines into textures

R2's `reussir_rt::rcgmp::Mpz` retains and releases through the rcgmp
runtime's C entry points, so every clone and drop of a `Nat` in a Rust
container is a call, also for an immediate, where retain and release do
nothing. lean2rr's typed `Array Nat` pays it on every read, copy and free:
a microbenchmark of push, sum, in-place bump and 100 copy-on-write copies
of 100k elements ran 2.03x the instructions of lean2rr's old Nat, about 8
instructions per element per call against 1 for the old inline bit test.

`Mpz`'s `Clone`/`Drop` now call `__reussir_rcgmp_ffi_acquire` and
`__reussir_rcgmp_ffi_release` as plain `extern "C"` functions. Rendering
`Mpz` (full/ffi.rs) records a glue entry keyed `__reussir_rcgmp` next to
the shared-record wrappers; it declares nothing in the texture and needs
no comparison bridge, and monomorphization turns it into an `FfiRcGlue`
pair with those two symbols, like a shared record's
`<sym>_ffi_acquire`/`_ffi_release`. Monomorphization also adds the pair to
every program that uses Nat or Int (`uses_bigint`, the condition under
which rrc links the rcgmp runtime) when no texture asked for it, so
runtime code outside the textures that holds an `Mpz` (lean2rr's runtime
keeps big Nats in box cells) always finds it in a program with integers.
Codegen (`ffi_rc_glue`) emits their bodies as `rcgmp.retain` and
`rcgmp.release` instead of `rc.inc`/`rc.dec`, in the home unit and
`weak_odr` under every policy (one fixed name, defined alike by every
module), and the backend gives every `__reussir_rcgmp_ffi_*` definition
hidden visibility once the module is linked (`hide_rcgmp_glue` in
reussir-backend's llvm.rs), so that each module and shared library binds
its own copy, next to its own view of the rcgmp runtime. So the rcgmp
lowering decides the immediate test (the dialect does not make its tag an
ABI; no tag in Rust), and LLVM inlines the glue into the linked texture
code wherever their target attributes allow it, as for lean2rr's
textures, which are compiled for the program's CPU.

reussir-rt has no fallback and no weak symbols, and builds on stable Rust:
`Mpz`'s methods are `#[inline]`, compiled only into code that uses them,
so the runtime library references neither the glue nor the rcgmp runtime,
and a program without integers links without the rcgmp runtime and GMP.
Runtime code outside the textures that a program without integers links,
and that holds an `Mpz` in dead code, needs `--gc-sections` (the default
of rustc's and rrc's links) to drop the reference to the glue.

Also drops "hashed" from the rcgmp module doc: Mpz has no Hash.

Instruction counts (cachegrind) of 1000 copy-on-write copies of a
1000-element Vec<Nat>, textures compiled for the host CPU as lean2rr does:
immediates 30.86M (R2) to 12.85M, heap values 41.08M to 26.07M. With
textures for rustc's generic target, which keeps the glue out of line,
17.86M and 28.08M.

Tests: new frontend/bigint_ffi_glue.rr (the glue's MLIR, its MIR round
trip, its hidden weak_odr definitions also in a program without textures
(bigint_division.rr) and none in a program without integers
(ffi_vec_e2e.rr), and at -O with the textures' target no call to the glue
or to rcgmp's retain/release in a Vec<Nat> copy-and-drop loop, a clone or
a drop, with each function's checks as its own prefix; the program runs),
new frontend/bigint_ffi_dynlib.rr (a Reussir shared library with Nat
textures and its own static runtimes, in a host that links
libreussir_rt.so ahead of it: it runs and exports none of the glue), ASan
runs of bigint_ffi_glue.rr and of bigint_ffi_generic.rr with the glue
inlined in sanitizer/bigint-ffi-generic-asan.rr, the glue pair in the
reussir-core unit test, docs/design/polymorphic-ffi.md.
