+`Clone` and `Drop` call the glue `__reussir_rcgmp_ffi_acquire` and
+`__reussir_rcgmp_ffi_release`, which monomorphization requests whenever a
+texture spells `Mpz` or the program otherwise uses `Nat` or `Int`, and codegen
+emits like a shared record's rc glue, in the home unit, with the bodies
+`rcgmp.retain` and `rcgmp.release`: `weak_odr` under every policy, and hidden
+(`reussir_backend::llvm` marks every `__reussir_rcgmp_ffi_*` definition), so
+that each linked module and shared library binds its own copy, next to its
+own view of the rcgmp runtime. The rcgmp lowering decides the immediate test,
+which keeps the dialect's tag out of the runtime crate, and LLVM inlines the
+glue into the linked texture code where their target attributes allow (a
+texture compiled for the same CPU as the program): cloning or dropping an
+immediate is a bit test, and only a heap integer's count is touched. Runtime
+code outside the textures that holds an `Mpz` (a host runtime's own
+containers) resolves the glue against the program it is linked into, which
+defines it because it uses integers. The methods are inlined into the code
+that uses them, so `reussir-rt` references neither the glue nor the rcgmp
+runtime, and a program without integers links without them.
 
